Human: Can you tell me the steps to write an if/then statement in C programming language?

Assistant: Let me try!  When you meet with an “if” or “then” directive in C, for example, you want to check whether an input flag has been set and, if so, what should happen or do you next.  I want to give three basic directions to follow:

The basic direction is: if the flag is set, do this, but do not ignore it.  An example would be:

if (flagSet()) {
  output("This is what needs to happen!")
} else {
  output("Do nothing")
}

This says “if the flag is already set, do this.”  

A second general direction is to check for different combinations of flags and see what happens based on that.  To give an example:

if (flag1 && flag2) {
  do_something();
} else if (another_flag) {
  do_another_something();
} else {
  output("Whatever happened next, happened next.")
}

This says “if the flags have all been cleared, or any flags at all have been set, do something.  If any flags have only been cleared, do something else.  If none of these things happen, do your normal, final output.

The third direction is slightly special in C: if some combination of flags and not enough of them are set, just do this.  This makes sense for any situation, from saying something polite to saying something less polite, as it’s always good to be polite.  “If a flag is clear and only the flag to do this was set, do this.”  An example of this is:

if ((flag1 && flag2) && flag3 && flag4; ) {
  output("What are we doing here?")
} else {
  output("Whatever comes next, comes next.")
}
